# ULX Component Philosophy

## Ember Version

- **Use Ember 6.8.** All component code must be valid for and follow idioms of Ember 6.8 (e.g. Glimmer Components, `@tracked`, `@action`, modern helpers/modifiers).

## Paths

- **Component source**: `src/components`

## PrimeReact as Reference Only

- **MAY** use PrimeReact as a reference for: behavior, public API shape, interaction patterns, props/arguments, and a11y.
- **MUST** implement everything as **native Ember/Glimmer** components — no PrimeReact dependency.
- **MUST** align with Ember 6.8 patterns: Glimmer reactivity, `@tracked` / `@action`, Ember routing and services as needed.

## How to Use PrimeReact

- Use PrimeReact docs to learn: which arguments to expose, how the component should behave, which a11y features to support.
- Implement those ideas in Ember (e.g. if PrimeReact has `activeIndex`, you might have `@activeItem` and implement with `@tracked` and `@action`).

## Rules

- All components live under `src/components` and are built from scratch in Ember.
- No PrimeReact (or other React) code or imports in the codebase.
- **Class prefix**: Use `NAMESPACE` from `src/utils/component-config.js` for the component class prefix. Prefer `getComponentClass(componentName)` from that file when building class names (e.g. `getComponentClass('button')` → `ulx-button`). Do not hardcode prefix strings like `ulx-` or `ulx-` in components.
- **Avoid Ember 6.8 deprecations**: Do not use deprecated APIs. Prefer: Glimmer Components (`@glimmer/component`) and `this.args`; `@tracked` for state; `@action` or `fn` for callbacks; `{{on}}` from `@ember/modifier` for DOM events. Avoid: `Ember.Component`, `Ember.get` / `Ember.set`, `computed()`, `observer()`, `@ember/object` get/set, `sendAction`, classic lifecycle hooks (`didInsertElement` → use modifiers or `{{did-insert}}`).
- Always use _@action_ for methods and functions
- **Component structure**: Use class-based Glimmer components with an embedded template (`.gjs`). Put elements in `src/components/elements/`, collections in `src/components/collections/`, modules in `src/components/modules/`.
- **Arguments**: Document public API with JSDoc `@param` on the component class. Use `this.args.*` or getters that read from `this.args`; do not mutate `this.args`.
- **Named blocks**: Named blocks **MUST** start with lowercase (e.g. `<:icon>`, `<:header>`, not `<:Icon>`). This is required in Ember for `.gjs` and strict mode: block names are treated as regular identifiers, and uppercase names can conflict with component/helper resolution.
- **Accessibility**: When using PrimeReact as reference, implement the same a11y behavior (ARIA attributes, keyboard support, focus management) in the Ember component.
- **Avoid** Don't add any comments related to primereact
- **Must** In the demo page, for the severity case use instead that as Variant not severity
- Avoid writing new size classes
- **Must** Always use @action for the function used in the template
- **MUST** Use ember-truth-helpers if needed and dont forget to import that
- **Must** Use 'i18n t helper' for all the strings used in the component.Import the t helper in the component also
- **Must** Also must use the components like input ,button etc... if needed use the existing components we have. Eg ulxbutton, ulxicon, ulxinput....

## Code Style Patterns

All components **MUST** follow these code style patterns for consistency and readability:

### Argument Destructuring

- **Destructure `this.args` with defaults** in getters for better readability and maintainability.
- Use default values directly in destructuring assignments to eliminate conditional checks.
- Destructure within getters to maintain Glimmer reactivity (do not override `this.args`).
- **MUST** Variables should be more specific and meaningful.

```javascript
// ✅ CORRECT: Destructure with defaults in getter
get rootClasses() {
  const {
    variant = "primary",
    size = "s-size",
    disabled = false,
    customClass
  } = this.args;

  // Use destructured variables...
}

// ❌ WRONG: Accessing this.args directly throughout
get rootClasses() {
  if (this.args.variant) {
    // ...
  }
}
```

### Conditional Logic

- **Use ES6+ syntax** for conditional operations. Prefer logical AND (`&&`) for conditional pushes/assignments.
- Use nullish coalescing (`??`) for fallback values when appropriate.

```javascript
// ✅ CORRECT: Modern ES6+ syntax
dot && parts.push('dot');
disabled && parts.push('disabled');
customClass && parts.push(customClass);
const value = this.args.value ?? defaultValue;

// ❌ WRONG: Verbose if statements
if (dot) {
	parts.push('dot');
}
if (disabled) {
	parts.push('disabled');
}
```

### Class Building Pattern

- When building class strings, destructure args with defaults first, then build the class array.
- Use logical AND for conditional class additions.
- Filter and join at the end for clean, readable code.

```javascript
get rootClasses() {
  const {
    variant = "primary",
    size = "s-size",
    disabled = false,
    customClass
  } = this.args;

  const parts = [this.baseClass];
  parts.push(variant);
  parts.push(size);
  disabled && parts.push("disabled");
  customClass && parts.push(customClass);

  return [...new Set(parts.filter(Boolean))].join(" ");
}
```

## Component Design Principles

Best components are:

- **Argument-driven**: Components receive data and behavior through arguments (`this.args`), not internal state or external dependencies.
- **Stateless by default**: Prefer components that derive their output from arguments without maintaining internal state.
- **No lifecycle hooks**: Avoid component lifecycle hooks that introduce side effects or complexity.
- **No side effects**: Components should be pure functions of their arguments, rendering output without side effects.

❌ **Avoid**:

- `did-insert`
- `did-update`
- `will-destroy`
- Constructors
- Observers
- Mixins
- Don't create any .md files when component is created.

**If you need effects → use resources or modifiers, not components.**

## WCAG compliance

All components **MUST** meet WCAG requirements. Apply these rules so every component can pass accessibility review.

### General

- **Decorative vs meaningful**: For purely decorative content, use `aria-hidden="true"` so assistive tech skips it. For meaningful content (icons that convey info, images that matter), expose a name via `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby`.
- **Overrides**: Prefer passing a11y attributes through `...attributes` so callers can set `aria-label`, `role`, etc. when needed. Default to safe choices (e.g. decorative) and let callers override.
- **Keyboard & focus**: Interactive components must be keyboard operable and have visible focus. Use `tabindex`, `role`, and focus management as appropriate for the control type.
- **Semantics**: Use the right HTML element or ARIA role (e.g. `role="button"` only when it behaves like a button; use `<button>` when possible).
- **snippets**: we need to write whatever the code demo page variation is having , it should be present in the snippet file also, including the imports.

### Icon components

Icon components (e.g. UlxIcon) **MUST** follow:

- **iconname** Must use the icon from this https://cdn.zicons.in/21598000000025464/latest/bs-icons1.css cdn.If the icon is not present just let it empty.
- **`aria-hidden="true"`** when the icon is decorative (default). Ensures screen readers ignore it when it doesn’t add information.
- **`aria-label`** when the icon is meaningful. Callers pass it via `...attributes` (e.g. `<UlxIcon aria-label="Close" />`). Do not add a default `aria-label`; only use it when the caller provides one.
- **Suggestions**:
  - For inline/custom SVG, set `focusable="false"` on the `<svg>` when the icon is non-interactive to avoid focus quirks in some browsers.
  - If the icon is the only content of a button or link, the button/link must have an accessible name (e.g. `aria-label` on the control, or visually hidden text), not only on the icon.
  - Document in the component JSDoc when to use `aria-label` and how `...attributes` affects a11y (e.g. “Decorative by default; for meaningful icons pass `role="img"` and `aria-label`.”).
